<h2>The situation</h2>

<p>
  Healthcare practices in local markets share a problem with senior living
  communities: the person searching is often not the person who will become the
  patient or resident. An adult child researches on behalf of a parent. A spouse
  researches before the other spouse is ready to admit there's an issue. The
  search journey is long, emotionally weighted, and heavily influenced by trust
  signals that have nothing to do with keyword density.
</p>

<p>
  Practices also operate across wildly different market types. The same chain
  might have a location in a dense metro competing against a dozen rivals and
  another in a rural county where the realistic competitor set is two.
  Treating those identically produces mediocre results in both.
</p>

<h2>What I did</h2>

<h3>Keyword clustering mapped to the real funnel</h3>
<p>
  Rather than a flat keyword list, I cluster by intent stage &mdash; symptom
  research, solution research, provider comparison, and ready-to-book &mdash;
  and then map each cluster to the page type that should own it. This
  consistently surfaces a gap: practices tend to over-invest in bottom-of-funnel
  "near me" terms and under-invest in the research-stage questions where the
  family actually starts.
</p>

<h3>Competitive content gap analysis per market</h3>
<p>
  Share of voice is market-specific. I analyze what is actually ranking in each
  location's SERP rather than against a national competitor set, which changes
  the recommendation substantially. In a rural market the opportunity is often
  a straightforward title tag and local relevance fix. In a saturated metro it
  requires genuine content depth and differentiation.
</p>

<h3>Title tag strategy tuned for smaller markets</h3>
<p>
  One pattern worth stating plainly: the title tag formula that works for a
  competitive metro practice frequently underperforms in a rural or smaller
  market, where including the specific town and a plain-language service term
  outperforms the more polished brand-forward construction. I've written about
  this approach publicly because it's a repeatable, low-cost win that a lot of
  multi-location programs miss by standardizing too aggressively.
</p>

<h3>Google Business Profile at portfolio scale</h3>
<p>
  GBP is where a meaningful share of local healthcare discovery actually
  resolves, and it breaks in ways that have nothing to do with the website.
  I handle profile optimization, category and service accuracy, and the
  troubleshooting that comes with managing profiles across many locations &mdash;
  including diagnosing interface and display glitches that make a profile look
  broken to the practice owner.
</p>

<h3>Controlling what the SERP shows</h3>
<p>
  On one engagement a negative review was being pulled into the search result
  snippet for a hearing aid retailer &mdash; the first thing a prospective
  patient saw. I diagnosed how the snippet was being generated and resolved it
  so the result displayed intended content instead. Snippet control is an
  underused lever: the ranking position was never the problem, the presentation
  was.
</p>

<h2>Why this transfers to senior living</h2>

<p>
  The structural similarities are strong: multi-location, locally-searched,
  long consideration cycle, family and caregiver decision-makers, high-value
  conversions measured in tours and move-ins rather than transactions, and a
  trust threshold that content has to clear before a form gets filled. The
  tactics that work for a hearing practice in a small market are the same
  tactics that work for a community trying to reach families in its county.
</p>
